        "example": "Tesla (2020): Strengths: brand, tech lead. Weaknesses: production scaling, cash burn. Opportunities: EV mandates, battery cost decline. Threats: legacy OEMs catching up. | Netflix (2023): Strengths: 230M subs, content library. Weaknesses: rising content costs, password sharing crackdown backlash. Opportunities: ads tier, gaming. Threats: Disney+ bundling. | Local restaurant: Strengths: chef reputation, loyal regulars. Weaknesses: no delivery, small marketing budget. Opportunities: UberEats partnership, catering. Threats: chain restaurant opening nearby.",
        "why_it_matters": "SWOT is the most widely used strategy framework — and the most frequently misused. Done right, SWOT forces disciplined separation between internal factors (Strengths/Weaknesses — you control) and external factors (Opportunities/Threats — you don't). The key is pairing Strengths with Opportunities (S-O strategies) and Weaknesses with Threats (W-T strategies). These pairings are where strategy lives.",
