        "definition": "The architecture of an acquisition: cash vs stock, earnouts, holdbacks, representations & warranties, indemnification, closing conditions. Structure often matters more than price.",
        "example": "Cash deal: $100M at close. Clean, simple. Seller bears no future risk. | Earnout: $60M at close + $40M if targets hit over 3 years. Aligns incentives, protects buyer. | Stock deal: Buyer issues shares instead of cash. Seller's risk tied to buyer's future performance. | Indemnification cap: Seller's liability capped at 15% of deal value. Anything above = buyer's risk. Anything below = seller's reps & warranties insurance.",
