        "name": "Captive Insurance",
        "definition": "A wholly-owned insurance subsidiary that insures the parent company. Advantages: 1) Keep underwriting profits (don't pay them to a commercial insurer) 2) Cover risks the market won't touch 3) Tax benefits (premiums paid to captive are tax-deductible, captive's reserves grow tax-advantaged). Typically makes sense at $1M+ in annual premiums.",
        "example": "Fortune 500 captives: 90% of Fortune 500 have captive insurers. Shell, BP, and others use captives to cover environmental liability, political risk, and supply chain risks that commercial markets either won't cover or charge extreme premiums for. | Mid-market captive: company paying $800k/year in premiums. Form a captive in Vermont or Bermuda. Captive issues policies, parent pays premiums to captive. Captive invests reserves. Underwriting profits stay in the group. | Feasibility test: premiums > $1M, predictable loss history, management bandwidth to run the captive. If premiums < $500k, the administrative overhead exceeds the benefit.",
