    "id": "",
    "name": "Technology S-Curve",
    "definition": "Technologies follow an S-curve: slow initial progress, rapid improvement, then diminishing returns as limits are reached. Timing the jump to the next S-curve is the CEO's job.",
    "formula": null,
    "example": "Hard disk drives: 14-inch → 8-inch → 5.25-inch → 3.5-inch. Each new format started worse than the optimized old format, then surpassed it. Incumbents missed each transition. | Electric vehicles: ICE engines at top of S-curve (diminishing returns on efficiency). Battery tech climbing steep part of S-curve (rapid improvement). | CEO's task: Monitor when current technology approaches its limits. Invest in next S-curve BEFORE the current one peaks. Too early = wasted investment. Too late = extinction.",
    "tags": [
      "innovation",
      "technology"
    ],
