    "id": "",
    "name": "Theory of Constraints",
    "definition": "Eliyahu Goldratt: Every system has ONE bottleneck that limits throughput. Improve the bottleneck, improve the whole system. Improve anything else = wasted effort.",
    "formula": null,
    "example": "Factory: Machine #3 is the bottleneck (15 units/hour, while others do 25). Adding capacity to Machine #1 does nothing — parts just pile up at Machine #3. Fix the bottleneck first. | 5 focusing steps: 1) IDENTIFY constraint (Machine #3) 2) EXPLOIT it (no idle time, preventive maintenance) 3) SUBORDINATE everything else (don't feed Machine #3 faster than 15/hr) 4) ELEVATE the constraint (add second Machine #3) 5) REPEAT — now find the new bottleneck. | Software team: If QA is the bottleneck, having developers code faster just increases the testing backlog. Fix QA capacity first.",
    "tags": [
      "operations",
      "constraints"
    ],
