    "id": "",
    "name": "Just-in-Time (JIT)",
    "definition": "Inventory strategy: materials arrive exactly when needed in production, not before. Reduces carrying costs, reveals process problems (no buffer inventory hides issues), and forces quality improvements.",
    "formula": null,
    "example": "Toyota: Parts arrive 4 hours before assembly. No warehouse. If a part is defective, production stops — the problem is visible immediately and gets fixed. | Dell in 1990s: Build-to-order model. No finished goods inventory. Customer orders, Dell builds. Components arrive JIT. Cash conversion cycle was NEGATIVE (got paid before paying suppliers). | Risk: JIT breaks when supply chains break. COVID showed this — 2-week inventory buffer vs 2-hour. Balance JIT with strategic buffers for critical components.",
