    "name": "Spider Charts",
    "definition": "A radar-style chart showing how multiple input variables affect the output when each is varied across its range. The variable with the steepest line has the most impact — visually obvious in seconds.",
    "formula": null,
    "example": "Five variables plotted: lines from center to edge. Churn rate line is nearly vertical (huge impact). CAC line is gentle slope (moderate). Office rent line is flat (negligible). Board sees instantly: churn dominates. | Better than tornado charts for showing RELATIVE sensitivity of many variables simultaneously. Use both: tornado for ranking, spider for pattern recognition. | Spider chart reveals interactions: if two lines cross, those variables interact. Investigate the crossing point.",
