    "name": "Shareholder Letter Writing",
    "definition": "The annual shareholder letter is your direct channel to owners. Buffett writes his himself — it's the most widely read business document in the world. Write honestly about mistakes, explain the business in plain English, and communicate the long-term vision.",
    "formula": null,
    "example": "Amazon 1997 letter: 'It's Day 1.' Simple, bold, set the long-term philosophy. Bezos attached this letter to EVERY subsequent annual report for 20+ years. | Buffett's letters: admit mistakes freely ('I was wrong about X'). Explain insurance float in simple terms. Use humor. Result: trust that survives market cycles. | Bad shareholder letter: jargon-filled, corporate-speak, blames 'macroeconomic headwinds' for everything, never admits mistakes. Zero credibility built.",
