    "name": "Risk Appetite Statements",
    "definition": "A board-approved document declaring: 1) How much risk the company is willing to take 2) In what areas 3) Measured how 4) With what limits. Not 'we're risk-aware' — specific thresholds that trigger action.",
    "formula": null,
    "example": "Risk appetite: 'We accept up to $2M in uncovered operational risk per year. We will not accept any single risk that could reduce liquidity below $10M. We accept cybersecurity risk up to the point of a data breach affecting more than 50,000 customers.' | Risk tolerance (narrower): 'For customer data breach risk, we tolerate a maximum 10% annual probability of an event exceeding $1M.' | Every material risk should have an appetite statement. Without one, the organization doesn't know when to escalate, and risk management is reactive.",
    "tags": [
      "risk",
      "governance"
    ],
