    "name": "FAIR Model (Breach Quantification)",
    "definition": "Factor Analysis of Information Risk: the standard for quantifying cyber risk in dollars. Loss Event Frequency × Loss Magnitude = Annualized Loss Expectancy (ALE). Turns 'our cyber risk is high' into 'our cyber risk is $3.2M/year expected loss.'",
    "formula": null,
    "example": "Scenario: ransomware attack on customer database. Loss Event Frequency: 0.15 (15% chance/year). Loss Magnitude: P10=$1M, P50=$4M, P90=$12M. ALE = 0.15 × $4M = $600k/year. | Cost of mitigation: $200k/year for improved backup + $100k for training = $300k/year. ROI = ($600k - $300k)/$300k = 100%. Clear investment case. | Board question: 'Should we pay for cyber insurance?' Run FAIR analysis with and without insurance. Show the risk reduction for $150k premium. FAIR makes cyber risk a business conversation, not a technical one.",
    "tags": [
      "risk",
      "cyber"
    ],
