    "id": "",
    "name": "Ransomware Readiness",
    "definition": "The single most likely cyber event for most companies. Readiness: 1) Immutable backups (3-2-1 rule) 2) Offline backup that ransomware can't reach 3) Pre-negotiated incident response firm on retainer 4) Ransom payment decision framework (board-approved policy BEFORE you're hit) 5) Tabletop exercise quarterly.",
    "formula": null,
    "example": "3-2-1 backup: 3 copies of critical data, 2 different media types (disk + tape), 1 copy offsite (air-gapped, not reachable from the network). Ransomware can't encrypt tapes on a shelf. | Ransom payment policy: 'We will not pay ransoms' — sounds principled, but what if the alternative is 4 weeks of downtime at $2M/week? Pre-decide with board. | Incident response retainer: firms like CrowdStrike, Mandiant, or Unit 42 on annual retainer ($50-100k/year). They respond within hours, negotiate with attackers if needed, handle forensics. You don't want to be googling 'ransomware response firm' during an incident.",
