    "name": "Counterparty Credit Risk",
    "definition": "When you deposit money in a bank or enter a derivative contract, you're exposed to that counterparty's creditworthiness. If they fail, you lose your deposit or your hedge. Manage by diversifying counterparties, monitoring their credit ratings, and using collateral agreements (CSA for derivatives).",
    "formula": null,
    "example": "SVB collapse 2023: thousands of startups had 100% of their cash at Silicon Valley Bank. When it failed, they couldn't make payroll. Lesson: diversify. | Diversification rule: no single bank should hold >25% of total cash. At least one bank should be systemically important (too-big-to-fail). | Derivatives: when you hedge FX with a forward contract, the bank's obligation to pay you is only as good as that bank. Use multiple banks for large hedges. | Credit Support Annex (CSA): the derivative counterparty posts collateral if their credit rating drops. Protects you.",
    "tags": [
      "risk",
      "financial"
    ],
    "order_index": 2,
    "why_it_matters": null,
